From a30f151e518c1158021c4303689af3bb78b99284 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:45:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision through the DisplayPort to HDMI
 converter of a board

The HDMI socket of many Intel mini PCs and NUCs of the 11th generation and later is behind a
DisplayPort to HDMI converter, which the driver calls a DisplayPort connector, and native output
was refused there ("converter"). The kernel (linux-9999-pcon) now gives a DisplayPort connector of
a port that can carry the tunnel through a converter DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB and
DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER, read only: none, unlisted, listed (the board is on its list of those where
it was seen working), forced (dv_pcon=1).

A converter that does not carry the Dolby vendor InfoFrame leaves the TV without Dolby Vision or
without a picture, so Kodi offers the tunnel through it by default only for listed and forced, as
the new expert setting videoscreen.hadesdvconverter says: Automatic (that), On (any converter that
the kernel takes: the user takes the risk) or Off (never, even on the list). Where no converter is
behind the port, or the property is not there, nothing changes: the refusal is the one of before.
The player information says why it is not offered ("converteroff", "converterunlisted"), the
status line of native output says through what it goes, and kodi.log says what the connector
reports when the output is taken. The setting is shown only on an output that has the property and
is read when a video starts, with the others.

Decision in DV_CONVERTER (DVConverter.h), host test in both directions (23 checks and two
mutations that it catches). Strings 41300-41304 (core) and 32900-32906 (Estuary), in English and
Spanish. Not tried on hardware. The idea and the first board are from YBLOD (linux-9930).
